  "text": "The last train to Osaka left without its driver. It was a Tuesday, unremarkable in every way except for the silence that followed the departure. The platform at Kyoto Station was empty, save for a single vending machine humming its low, electric lullaby. Kaito stood frozen, his coffee cup still warm in his hand, watching the taillights of the Shinkansen shrink into the distance. He had been the conductor, or at least, he had been the one holding the clipboard, the one who checked the doors, the one who ensured the passengers were seated. But when the whistle blew, he had not been in the cab. He had been on the platform, staring at his reflection in the glass, seeing not a man, but a ghost of a routine. The train moved with a smooth, predatory grace, carrying hundreds of souls toward the city, yet it felt hollow, a vessel without a heart. Kaito’s mind raced, trying to reconstruct the last ten minutes. Had he fallen asleep? Had he been pushed? The memories were fragmented, like shards of broken glass, sharp and painful. He looked down at his hands; they were trembling. The coffee sloshed, spilling hot liquid onto his trousers, but he didn’t feel the burn. He only felt the cold void where his purpose should have been. The train was gone, swallowed by the night, and with it, his identity. He was no longer the conductor. He was just a man standing on a platform, waiting for a train that would never come, or perhaps, waiting for himself to return."
